# 6. Policy Option 4 — Establish Ecological-Scale Conservation Compacts

## Problem

Many conservation problems cross administrative boundaries.

Individual states or agencies may have strong programs while lacking sufficient authority to address the full ecological system.

## Policy concept

Expand the use of voluntary or statutory conservation compacts organized around ecological systems rather than political jurisdictions.

Potential applications include:

* river basins;
* migration corridors;
* large mammal ranges;
* shared wildlife landscapes;
* coastal ecosystems;
* and multi-state habitat networks.

Compacts could establish:

* shared objectives;
* common indicators;
* joint monitoring;
* coordinated management;
* funding agreements;
* dispute-resolution mechanisms;
* and periodic performance reviews.

## Advantages

* Aligns governance with ecological boundaries.
* Preserves state and Tribal authority.
* Improves coordination.
* Can be tailored to specific ecosystems.
* May be more politically and administratively feasible than national centralization.

## Risks

* Participation may be uneven.
* Agreements can become difficult to maintain.
* Institutions may disagree over priorities.
* Voluntary arrangements may lack enforcement mechanisms.
* Some ecological systems cross too many jurisdictions for compact governance to be practical.

## Research requirement

The project should identify existing successful examples of interstate and intergovernmental conservation agreements before proposing a national model.

