# 15. Institutional Architecture

The proposed framework does not require a single centralized conservation authority.

Instead, responsibilities could remain distributed.

### Federal government

* national standards;
* interstate and international issues;
* ESA implementation;
* national monitoring coordination;
* federal lands;
* scientific infrastructure;
* funding;
* data interoperability.

### States

* resident wildlife;
* state lands;
* hunting and fishing management;
* local implementation;
* State Wildlife Action Plans;
* population monitoring.

### Tribal governments

* sovereign wildlife management;
* treaty rights;
* cultural resources;
* co-management;
* Indigenous ecological knowledge;
* habitat and species conservation.

### Local governments

* land use;
* development planning;
* local habitat;
* stormwater and watershed management;
* parks and open space.

### Private landowners

* habitat stewardship;
* working-land conservation;
* voluntary conservation agreements;
* restoration.

### Universities and scientific institutions

* research;
* independent evaluation;
* monitoring;
* modeling;
* training.

### Nonprofit organizations

* restoration;
* land protection;
* research;
* implementation;
* public engagement;
* technical assistance.

The objective is not to eliminate this distributed architecture.

It is to make the connections between its components more effective.

