# 16. What Should Not Be Assumed

The evidence does not currently justify several common assumptions.

### More federal authority is automatically better

Not necessarily.

Centralization could improve coordination in some circumstances while reducing local knowledge or creating administrative rigidity in others.

### More protected land automatically means better wildlife outcomes

Not necessarily.

Protected land can be ecologically valuable while still failing to address the limiting factor for a particular species.

### More funding automatically produces better conservation

Not necessarily.

Funding matters, but the relationship depends on implementation capacity, intervention quality, ecological constraints, and targeting.

### Every listed species needs the same conservation approach

Clearly not.

Species differ substantially in biology, threats, habitat, geographic range, and management requirements.

### Every delisted species represents an equally successful conservation model

No.

The mechanisms producing recovery can differ substantially, and some recovered species may remain dependent on continuing management.

